> \]”

*Correct.*  The set \(S_n\) is exactly the collection of required points, and the count \(\frac{n(n+1)}2\) follows from the well‑known triangular number formula.

---

### 2.  Reduction to the triangle \(T_k\)

> “Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.  A vertical line covers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) can meet a column in at most one point.  Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \(S_n\) and we have exactly the same number \(k\) of sunny lines.  
> Consequently we may (and shall) assume that the \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  
> \[
> x=1,\;x=2,\dots ,x=n-k .
> \tag{1.1}
> \]”

*Issue (Justification Gap).*  
The claim that the replacement “still cover[s] every point of \(S_n\)” is not proved.  
A non‑vertical non‑sunny line (horizontal or slope \(-1\)) can indeed intersect each column in at most one point, but after it is removed those points in the other columns may no longer be covered by any line.  The argument that the remaining sunny lines automatically cover those lost points is omitted.  A rigorous proof would need to show that for any configuration one can perform the replacement while preserving coverage, e.g. by a constructive re‑assignment of the sunny lines or by an explicit inductive argument.  As written the step is a plausible “without loss of generality” claim, but it lacks a complete justification.

*Impact.*  The reduction is used throughout the rest of the solution; however, the final answer is still correct, so the flaw is a presentation gap rather than a fatal logical error.

---

### 3.  Description of the remaining points  

> “All points with abscissa \(a\le n-k\) are already covered.  The remaining points are  
