on; however, the final answer is still correct, so the flaw is a presentation gap rather than a fatal logical error.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Description of the remaining points  \n\n> \u201cAll points with abscissa \\(a\\le n-k\\) are already covered.  The remaining points are  \n> \\[\n> T_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n> \\tag{1.2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  After fixing the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\), the uncovered points are precisely those with \\(x>n-k\\).  Translating \\(x' = x-(n-k)\\) yields the set \\(T_k\\) as described.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Bounding the number of points a sunny line can contain in \\(T_k\\)\n\n> \u201cMultiplying its equation by a common denominator we obtain an integer equation  \n> \\[\n> p x-q y=c ,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n> \\tag{2.1}\n> \\]  \n> All integer points on the line are described by (3) above:  \n> \\[\n> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\tag{2.2}\n> \\]  \n> Their sums satisfy (4):  \n> \\[\n> x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n> \\tag{2.3}\n> \\]  \n> Because the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\); therefore the sum changes by at least \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) when \\(t\\) varies by one.  \n> Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  From (2.3) the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{2.4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue (Justification Gap).*  \nThe inequality \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) is valid only when \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  The case \\(|p+q|=1\\) (which corresponds to the lines with \\(p+q=\\pm1\\)) is not covered by this estimate.  The author later treats \\(|p+q|=1\\) separately, but the presentation leaves a gap: the reader must infer that the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor